<section class="article-header wrap-article">
    <p class="section-label">Opinion</p>
    <h1>The Government Is Pricing Workers Out of Jobs</h1>
    <div class="byline-row">
        <img src="/assets/images/grace-pariser-avatar.webp" alt="Grace Pariser" class="byline-avatar">
        <p class="byline">By Grace Pariser &middot; September 2026</p>
    </div>
</section>

<section class="section wrap-article" style="border-top:none;">
    <div class="prose dropcap">
        <p>On 25 August, the government's consultation on guaranteed hours closed. It asked businesses one question: how do you define "temporary need" for seasonal and project work? The question matters because the government already knows what its own reform will do to a business that takes on staff for three months over the summer and has nothing to offer them afterwards. It just hasn't decided how to stop that happening. Two more changes to how businesses employ people take effect on 1 October, and a fourth is already live: an employment tribunal system taking more claims than it ever has. None of this was designed to make hiring harder. Put together, that's what it does.</p>
        <p>The guaranteed hours reform is the clearest case of a good intention going wrong for employers. Anyone on a zero hours or low hours contract who works regularly over a reference period, likely twelve weeks though even that isn't settled yet, has to be offered a permanent contract reflecting those hours. Fair enough, on its own: the aim is to stop businesses using casual contracts to dodge the security a regular worker deserves, and some have done exactly that. But a business with genuinely seasonal work, running eleven or thirteen weeks rather than twelve, is left with two choices. Offer permanent hours it can't sustain once the season ends. Or manage someone's hours down, or end the arrangement early, so the obligation never triggers. Most will take the second option, because it's the only one that doesn't put next year's hiring at risk, and it's worse for the worker the policy meant to protect.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="pull-quote">
        <p>None of this was designed to make hiring harder. Put together, that's what it does.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="prose">
        <p>More changes are close behind that reform. From 1 October, employers become liable for right to work breaches committed by a subcontractor's staff, even where the person has never been on their own payroll (an agency-supplied security guard is the obvious example). The same day, every new starter has to get a standalone document, in specified wording, telling them they can join a trade union, before their first day. Manageable alone, both of them. Set them beside the existing duty to record working hours and holiday for every employee every week, and a minimum wage regime where fifteen unpaid minutes over the week is a criminal offence rather than a fine, and it's a genuinely complicated set of obligations for a business that just wants someone to answer the phones.</p>
        <p>I make my living advising on exactly this kind of complexity, so take what follows with a pinch of salt: none of these protections is unreasonable by itself. Workers should know they can join a union. Businesses should answer for who works on their premises, even through a subcontractor. No one should be short-changed on minimum wage by fifteen minutes or fifteen pounds. I don't disagree with a single one of these principles, taken on its own. Reasonable, isn't it? The trouble starts once you look at everything at once.</p>
        <p>No one in government looks at all of this together. Employers deal with it alone. Employing someone in the UK was already expensive before National Insurance went up this year, and every one of these changes makes it more expensive, riskier, or both, on top of a wage bill that was rising anyway. A rational employer facing that doesn't respond by treating staff better. It hires fewer of them, leans harder on contractors who sit outside some of these obligations, or automates a role rather than filling it. What it takes to employ someone changed, and the decision changed with it (most employers I work with want to do right by their people, but a rising wage bill beats good intentions every time).</p>
        <p>The claims side makes things worse. Tribunal claims are up, helped along by press coverage of employment rights that's barely let up since the election, and by workers turning to AI to help build a case, which brings its own problem given how often that advice is wrong. A business can follow every rule correctly and still face a claim, and once it does, it rarely makes sense to fight it. Legal fees to defend a claim properly can run to thirty thousand pounds. Settling for a fraction of that, with no admission of wrongdoing, is simply cheaper, whatever the claim's merits actually were. That's a poor outcome for the worker with a genuine grievance. The business writes off a cheque because it's cheaper than a trial, and every employer watching learns the same lesson: hiring is where the risk sits.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="pull-quote">
        <p>That's a poor outcome for the worker with a genuine grievance. Every employer watching learns the same lesson: hiring is where the risk sits.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="prose">
        <p>Each rule works as intended on its own. Employment reform is failing where it's treated as a series of separate announcements rather than one bill hitting an employer at the same time. A government that wants more people in work should look at everything it's already asking employers to do. It needs to do that before adding the next rule, not after the last consultation has closed.</p>
        <p>The reforms solve a political problem, being seen to strengthen workers' rights, rather than an employment problem, getting more people into work in the first place. The people paying for that gap are the ones who never get hired at all.</p>
    </div>
</section>

